\Babstract{
This paper covers defects in the design and wording of \ref("P4421R0").
}

\h2(listed=false){Contents}

\make_contents

\h2{Introduction}

\ref("P4421R0") \q{Numeric Traits for C++29}
proposes a number of new individually specializable numeric traits for C++29.
While the paper makes no novel design choices and
essentially just implements the existing LEWG design consensus,
it contains several design and wording problems that have never been addressed
and should be considered before any such paper is adopted.

Virtually all of these problems
are actually inherited defects from \tcode{numeric_limits} and its wording,
rather than new defects introduced by \ref("P4421R0").
However, we should always strive to improve the quality of design and wording
when introducing new facilities into the standard,
rather than perpetuating historical defects.

\h2{Design defects}

This section contains defects that are not purely wording issues
but require LEWG decisions.

\h3{\q{Subnormal}, not \q{denormalized}}

\b{Problem}:
The term \q{denormalized} is ancient and obsolescent.
It was replaced in IEEE-754:2008 (revising IEEE-754:1985);
since then, \q{denormalized} only appears as a synonym for \q{subnormal}.
The C++ standard has already adopted the new terminology
via macros like \tcode{FLT_HAS_SUBNORM} inherited from C and
possibly soon through \tcode{std::issubnormal} (see \ref("P3935R3")).

C++29 should adopt the new terminology consistently,
rather than copying the 1985 terminology that \tcode{numeric_limits} uses.

\b{Possible solution}:
Perform the following renaming:
\Bdiff{
\itemdecl{
template<class T> struct \del{de}\ins{sub}norm_min;
template<class T> constexpr auto \del{de}\ins{sub}norm_min_v;
}
}

\h3{\tt{min_exponent} is off by one}

\b{Problem}:
\tcode{min_exponent} is defined as follows:
\Bquote{
When enabled, with \tcode{r = radix_v<T>},
\tcode{min_exponent<T>::value} is
the minimum negative integer \math{\mi{i}} such that
\math{\msup{\mi{r}\mrow{\mi{i}\mo{−}\mn{1}}}}
is a representable, normalized (if applicable), finite value of type \tcode{T}.
}
The result is off by one or more, depending on the format:
\ul{
  \li{
    For ISO/IEC 60559 binary interchange floating-point types:
    \ul{
      \li{
        All subnormal numbers have an exponent of \math{\mi(mathvariant="italic"){emin}},
        which is \math{\mn{−126}} for \tcode{std::float32_t} (i.e. binary32 format).
        The magnitude of a subnormal number is
        \math{\mrow{
          \msup{\mn{2}\mi(mathvariant="italic"){emin}}
          \mo{×}
          \mn{0.abc…}
        }},
        where \math{\mn{abc…}} are digits decoded from the significand part.
      }
      \li{
        For normal numbers, the minimum exponent is the raw exponent \math{\mi{E}}
        minus a \math{\mi(mathvariant="italic"){bias}} constant
        (which is 127 for \tcode{std::float32_t}),
        and the minimum \math{\mrow{\mi{E}\mo{−}\math{\mi(mathvariant="italic"){bias}}}}
        is also \math{\mn{−126}} for \tcode{std::float32_t}
        because \math{\mi{E}} is at least \math{\mn{1}}.
        The magnitude of a normal number is
        \math{\mrow{
          \msup{\mn{2}\mrow{\mi{E}\mo{−}\math{\mi(mathvariant="italic"){bias}}}}
          \mo{×}
          \mn{1.abc…}
        }}
      }
    }
    However, \tcode{min_exponent} is \tcode{-125} for \tcode{std::float32_t}.
  }
  \li{
    For ISO/IEC 60559 decimal interchange floating-point types,
    subnormal values do not all have the same exponent,
    so \tcode{min_exponent} is off by potentially more than one.
  }
}

Essentially, \tcode{min_exponent} is neither the
\q{minimum exponent that would be multiplied with the significand part},
nor is it the \math{\mi(mathvariant="italic"){emin}} constant described in ISO/IEC 60559.
The result is inexplicably off by one because the definition is written in terms of
\math{\msup{\mi{r}\mrow{\mi{i}\mo{−}\mn{1}}}} instead of \math{\mi{r}}.
The proposed value does match \tcode{numeric_limits::min_exponent}
and the C89 macro \tcode{FLT_MIN_EXP},
but it's unclear why those have been designed as off by one in the first place.

What's even worse is that for decimal floating-point types,
\tcode{min_exponent} and \tcode{min_exponent10} yield different results;
\tcode{min_exponent} is asking about \math{\msup{\mn{10}\mrow{\mi{i}\mo{−}\mn{1}}}} while
\tcode{min_exponent10} is asking about \math{\msup{\mn{10}\mi{i}}}.

\b{Possible solution}:
We should not perpetuate the off-by-one error,
but standardize a trait that yields the expected \math{\mi(mathvariant="italic"){emin}} constant.

\h3{What is \tt{round_error}?!}

\b{Problem}:
The \tcode{round_error} trait is described as follows:
\Bquote{
When enabled, \tcode{round_error<T>::value} is \tcode{T}'s maximum rounding error.
}

This non-definition raises more questions than it answers.
What is a rounding error?
Which operations contribute to the rounding error?
Does that include \header{cmath} functions?
Are we talking about rounding errors on any scale?
Mysteriously, \tcode{numeric_limits::round_error} is \tcode{0.5}
for \tcode{float}, \tcode{double}, and \tcode{long double},
in practice.
What does that even mean?!
How can the \tcode{round_error} be independent of the floating-point type?!
Why is it \tcode{0.5} and how could anyone conclude that from the definition?!
Why is the \tcode{round_error} not infinite when rounding can result in infinitely large errors?

As it turns out,
the term comes from ISO/IEC 10967-1, \q{Language independent arithmetic},
also known as \q{LIA-1},
which C++ no longer normatively references.
C23 also removed all references to LIA-1 in favor of the ISO/IEC 60559 standard.
The concept of \q{rounding error} refers to the maximum difference from the mathematical result
when rounding a floating-point value using its configured rounding mode,
to an integer,
within the interval [0, 1].
When rounding to the nearest number (with correct rounding), that error is exactly 0.5;
when rounding toward negative or positive infinity, it is 1.0.

Overall, the trait name and wording are confusing,
and the trait as a whole refers to a somewhat arbitrary concept from LIA-1
that has no counterpart in C23.
It is unclear what anyone would do with the value of this trait,
except confuse themselves.
Assuming correct rounding,
the trait value can also be derived from \tcode{numeric_limits::round_style}
because it should always be \tcode{0.5} or \tcode{1.0}.

\tcode{round_error} may even be actively harmful to the language.
A GitHub code search for its uses
turned up only incorrect uses in which it is treated as some kind of
\q{epsilon} or \q{imprecision} value,
but that's not what it is;
\tcode{round_error} is entirely independent of the precision of the floating-point type.

\b{Possible solution}:
Remove \tcode{round_error} from the paper.

Ideally, deprecate \tcode{numeric_limits::round_error} as well,
or remove it from the standard,
but that would be outside the scope of \ref("P4421R0").

\h3{How do you check for subnormal numbers?}

\b{Problem}:
\ref("P4421R0") makes the following argument
\Bquote{
Generic numerical code can then ask,
at compile time and without a separate \tcode{has_*} query,
whether a given value or characteristic exists for a type \tcode{T},
and get an error rather than a meaningless default when it does not.
}

This design direction is contradicted by making the proposed \tcode{denorm_min} trait
default to \tcode{norm_min} when there are no normal numbers.
Other traits like \tcode{infinity} don't simply fall back onto e.g. the greatest finite value
but are disabled,
which explains why there is no dedicated \tcode{has_infinity} trait.

Although one could argue that it is not possible to reliably detect
whether the execution environment supports subnormal arithmetic,
the wording in \ref("P4421R0") explicitly states that this has no effect on the trait's result.
\Bquote{
\note{Whether the execution environment's floating-point operations
treat subnormal operands or results as zero at run time
has no effect on whether this specialization is enabled,
nor on the value of \tcode{denorm_min_v<T>}.}
}
Furthermore, it would be possible to check whether subnormals exist using
\tcode{std::issubnormal(\brk{}std::\brk{}denorm_min_v<float>)}
if \ref("P3935R3") were adopted,
but users should not be forced into this very silly workaround.
That code is essentially saying \tcode{std::is_apple(std::apple)}.

\b{Possible solution}:
Align \tcode{std::denorm_min} (hopefully renamed to \tcode{std::subnorm_min})
with the other traits, disabling it when the floating-point type has no subnormal numbers.

\Bnote{
The higher-level \q{closest positive number to zero} trait
can still be constructed in terms of these lower-level traits,
or in terms of \tcode{std::nextup(0)} with \ref("P3935R3"),
or in terms of \tcode{std::nextafter(0, 1)}.
}

\h3{\tt{denorm_min} and \tt{finite_min} use \q{min} inconsistently}

\b{Problem}:
\tcode{finite_min} is the lowest finite value,
which is typically a huge negative value.
\tcode{denorm_min} and \tcode{norm_min} instead use \tcode{min}
to refer to positive values only.

In other words, the term \tcode{min} is sometimes used to describe
the lowest value, and sometimes the value with lowest magnitude.

\b{Possible solution}:
Use \tcode{min} consistently in the trait names
or add \tcode{pos} to disambiguate (e.g. \tcode{pos_norm_min}).

\h2{Wording defects}

This section contains defects that are purely wording issues.

\h3{Unsigned infinity}

\b{Problem}:
C and C++ support types with an \q{unsigned infinity}.
For example,
\ref("https://cstd.eisie.net/c23.html#5.2.5.3.3p29"){C23 §5.2.5.3} states:
\Bquote{
The macro \tcode{INFINITY}
is defined if and only if the implementation supports an infinity for the type \tcode{float}.
It expands to a constant expression of type \tcode{float} representing
positive or unsigned infinity.
}
To be fair, formats with such infinities are largely historical.
Nonetheless, new wording could do more to accommodate unsigned infinity.

\b{Possible solution}:
Change the proposed wording as follows:
\Bdiff{
\itemdecl{
template<class T> struct infinity { \seebelow };
}

\itemdescr{
When enabled, \tcode{infinity<T>::value} is \tcode{T}'s positive
\ins{or unsigned}
infinity.
}
}

\h3{\q{Normal}, not \q{normalized}}

\b{Problem}:
The wording uses \q{denormalized} and \q{normalized} in a number of places.
For issues with \q{denormalized},
see \ref("#subnormal-not-denormalized").
Similarly, \q{normalized} is outdated.

\b{Possible solution}:
Replace uses of \q{normalized} with \q{normal},
which is the ISO/IEC 60559 standard terminology in its terms and definitions,
and which is consistent with \tcode{std::isnormal}.

\h3{\q{Significand}, not \q{mantissa}}

\b{Problem}:
The current standard term in ISO/IEC 60559 is \q{significand},
defined as follows:
\Bquote{
\dfn{significand}:
A component of a finite floating-point number containing its significant digits.
The significand can be thought of as an integer, a fraction, or some other fixed-point form,
by choosing an appropriate exponent offset.
A decimal or subnormal binary significand can also contain leading zeros,
which are not significant.
}

This term is used extensively in ISO/IEC 60559 and the C23 standard,
while \q{mantissa} is an ambiguous and non-standard term.
See also \ref("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Significand"){the Wikipedia article on Significand}.

Moreover, the context in which \q{mantissa} is used
in the proposed wording is problematic as well:

\Bquote{
\itemdecl{
template<class T> struct digits { \seebelow };
}
\itemdescr{
When enabled, \tcode{digits<T>::value} is the number of \tcode{radix_v<T>} digits
that can be represented without change.
If \tcode{is_integral_v<T>} is \tcode{true},
this is the number of non-sign bits in the representation;
if \tcode{is_floating_point_v<T>} is \tcode{true},
this is the number of \tcode{radix_v<T>} digits in the \mark{mantissa}.
}
}

First, we never defined what constitutes a \q{sign bit} for integral types;
for two's complement integers, there is no dedicated sign bit,
although the most significant bit indicates the sign of the number.

Second, for floating-point types,
the wording assumes, falsely,
that the number of bits dedicated to the significand is constant.
However, in formats such as ISO/IEC 60559 decimal interchange floating-point types,
that number varies:
sometimes the exponent has more bits, and sometimes the significand does,
because the \q{combination field} stores some bits of one or the other.

\b{Possible solution}:
Change the wording as follows:
\Bdiff{
When enabled, \tcode{digits<T>::value} is the number of \tcode{radix_v<T>} digits
that can be represented without change.

\ins{[\i{Note}:}
If \tcode{is_integral_v<T>} is \tcode{true},
this is the \del{number of non-sign bits in the representation;}
\ins{the width of the type, minus one if the type is signed.}
if \del{\tcode{is_floating_point_v<T>} is \tcode{true}}
\tcode{T} is an ISO/IEC 60559 binary interchange floating-point type,
this is the number of \tcode{radix_v<T>} digits in the \del{mantissa} significand
\ins{(including the leading implicit 1-bit)}.
\ins{\'EM DASH' \i{end note}]}
}

\h3{\tt{radix} for integers}

\b{Problem}:
The current wording for \tcode{radix} is somewhat vague
and relies on terms like \q{base of the representation}
that we never clearly define elsewhere.

While we still need to provide a mathematical explanation
to which user-defined specializations should adhere,
we should at least be precise in a note about the standard types.
We could also mention cases where the value is not \tcode{2},
such as ISO/IEC 60559 decimal interchange floating-point types.

\b{Possible solution}:
Change the wording as follows:
\Bdiff{
When enabled, \tcode{radix<T>::value} is the base of the representation.

\del{If is_floating_point_v<T> is true,
it is the base or radix (often 2) of the exponent representation.}
\ins{\note{
If \tcode{is_integral_v<T>} is \tcode{true}, it is \tcode{2}.
Otherwise, if \tcode{T} is a standard floating-point type\iref{basic.fundamental},
it is \tcode{FLT_RADIX}\iref{cfloat.syn}.
Otherwise, if \tcode{T} is one of the extended floating-point types
defined in header \header{stdfloat},
it is \tcode{2}.
Otherwise, if \tcode{T} is an ISO/IEC 60559 decimal interchange floating-point type,
it is \tcode{10}.
}}
}

